  === Complete Solutions
  #sol-item(1, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    General acceptability is the defining property. Government issue, paper form and metallic backing are characteristics of particular monies, not of money as such.
  ]

  #sol-item(2, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Medium of exchange. Barter requires that each party wants exactly what the other has; money breaks that requirement by being acceptable to everybody.
  ]
  ]

  #sol-item(3, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Unit of account — the common scale in which values are expressed and accounts kept, which is separate from the act of paying.
  ]

  #sol-item(4, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Inflation. If prices rise quickly, money held over time buys less, so it stores value badly compared with assets whose price rises with inflation.
  ]

  #sol-item(5, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Standard of deferred payment. A debt is fixed in a unit and repaid later, which requires that the unit be expected to exist and be accepted at repayment.
  ]

  #sol-item(6, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Liquidity. Cash is the most liquid asset precisely because it can be spent at once, which is also the reason it earns no return and loses value under inflation.
  ]

  #sol-item(7, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Cash can be exchanged for goods immediately and at no cost, so it is the most liquid. A fixed deposit, a bond and a house each take longer and cost more to convert.
  ]

  #sol-item(8, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Must be accepted in settlement of a debt. That is a legal property conferred by the state, and it has nothing to do with metallic backing or with interest.
  ]

  #sol-item(9, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Lending creates a deposit. Printing currency is the central bank's monopoly, borrowing from the central bank changes the banks' reserves rather than creating credit directly, and buying bills employs existing funds.
  ]

  #sol-item(10, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    1 ÷ 0.20 = 5. The ratio and the multiplier are reciprocals, and confusing them is the commonest error in the topic.
  ]

  #sol-item(11, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    ₦20,000 × (1 ÷ 0.25) = ₦20,000 × 4 = ₦80,000. ₦5,000 is the reserve, ₦20,000 applies no multiplier, and ₦100,000 divides by 0.20 instead of 0.25.
  ]

  #sol-item(12, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Reduce lending: a larger fraction of every deposit must be held idle, so the multiplier falls and fewer loans can be made.
  ]

  #sol-item(13, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Leaks reduce it. Cash held by the public and excess reserves held by banks both stop a loan from returning to the system as a fresh deposit, so the series converges sooner.
  ]

  #sol-item(14, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    It lends to a solvent bank in a liquidity crisis. That is what stops a run on one bank becoming a collapse of the system, and it is not about lending to government or to the public.
  ]

  #sol-item(15, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Accepting deposits from the general public is commercial banking. The central bank deals with the government and with banks, not with individuals.
  ]

  #sol-item(16, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The central bank, acting as banker to the government. Commercial and merchant banks serve firms and individuals, and a development bank funds specific sectors.
  ]

  #sol-item(17, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Buying and selling securities to drain or release liquidity. The reserve ratio is a separate instrument, and persuasion is moral suasion.
  ]

  #sol-item(18, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Sell securities and raise the reserve ratio — both drain liquidity and make credit dearer. The other pair is expansionary and would worsen the inflation.
  ]

  #sol-item(19, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Persuasion. It operates through the banks' willingness to cooperate rather than through a price or a rule, which is exactly why it is called moral.
  ]

  #sol-item(20, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    A sustained rise in the general price level. A single price rise is a relative price change, and a currency's external value is the exchange rate.
  ]

  #sol-item(21, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Demand-pull. Cost-push comes from the supply side, imported inflation arrives through the exchange rate, and structural inflation arises from bottlenecks in particular sectors.
  ]

  #sol-item(22, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Cost-push: costs rise, so aggregate supply shifts left, raising prices and reducing output.
  ]

  #sol-item(23, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Cutting demand lowers prices but also lowers output, which the economy is already short of. Demand-pull inflation can be damped without that same damage.
  ]

  #sol-item(24, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Debtors repay in money worth less than they borrowed. Fixed-income earners, bondholders and savers all receive sums fixed in nominal terms and lose real value.
  ]

  #sol-item(25, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Fixed incomes. A pension fixed in naira buys steadily less; borrowers, asset holders and traders able to reprice have some protection.
  ]

  #sol-item(26, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    A sustained fall in prices. A fall in the inflation rate is disinflation, a different phenomenon that is often confused with it.
  ]

  #sol-item(27, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Postpone spending. If goods will be cheaper next month, waiting pays, and the resulting fall in demand deepens the very deflation that caused the waiting.
  ]

  #sol-item(28, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    A proportional rise in the price level, if velocity and real output are constant. That is the classical statement of the link between money and prices.
  ]

  #sol-item(29, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The consumer price index follows the cost of a representative basket, and its percentage change is the headline inflation rate.
  ]

  #sol-item(30, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Discourage borrowing: dearer credit reduces investment and consumption. It raises rather than lowers the currency's attraction, and it has nothing to do with government spending.
  ]

  #sol-item(31, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Assets convertible into cash quickly and at little cost — treasury bills, savings deposits. Notes and coins are money proper rather than near money.
  ]

  #sol-item(32, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    More lending creates more deposits, which is more money. Selling securities, raising the reserve ratio and an increased preference for cash all reduce it.
  ]

  #sol-item(33, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The spread between deposit and lending rates. Printing currency is the central bank's monopoly and holding government accounts is its function.
  ]

  #sol-item(34, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    They borrow short and lend long, so no bank could repay every depositor at once. A run destroys a solvent bank by demanding that it do exactly that.
  ]

  #sol-item(35, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Cash. It can be spent instantly, which is liquidity, but inflation erodes its purchasing power over years, which is the store-of-value function failing.
  ]

  #sol-item(36, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    It rises from 4 (1 ÷ 0.25) to 5 (1 ÷ 0.20). A lower ratio leaves more of each deposit available to lend, so the system can support more credit.
  ]

  #sol-item(37, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Contractionary fiscal and monetary policy together: raise taxes, cut spending, sell securities. Every other combination contains an expansionary element that would worsen the excess demand.
  ]

  #sol-item(38, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Imported inflation. A cheaper currency makes imports dearer in domestic money, which raises costs and prices — a supply-side shock arriving through the exchange rate.
  ]

  #sol-item(39, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    A short-run trade-off that shifts with expectations. The curve is a menu that moves, which is precisely why it is not a law.
  ]

  #sol-item(40, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    A deposit is owed to the customer, so it is a liability. The other three are assets the bank owns or is owed.
  ]

]

